Abstract = {Two-dimensional (2-D) semiconductors offera pathway to extend Moore's
   law beyond silicon's fun-damental limits, leveraging their atomic-scale
   thicknessand inherent immunity to short-channel effects.
   However,integrating high-kgate dielectrics with 2-D semicon-ductors
   presents unique hurdles. The dangling-bond-free surface of 2-D
   semiconductors impedes uniformhigh-kdielectric growth; their atomically
   thin thicknessamplifies sensitivity to surface/interface defects,
   neces-sitating defect-minimizing integration processes. Thiswork
   comprehensively analyzes the critical challengesand recent advancements
   in achieving high-quality gatedielectric-semiconductor interfaces for
   2-D transistors,which are essential for their integration into
   scalable,energy-efficient integrated circuits. We systematically
   clas-sify and benchmark four state-of-the-art integration strate-gies.
   Notable advancements include ultralow interface statedensity D-it
   similar to 10(9 )cm(-2 )eV(-1 )achieved by damage-minimizingtechniques
   such as mechanical transfer. However, device-to-device nonuniformity
   remains a key challenge, withDitvarying by up to three orders of
   magnitude. Toadvance interface engineering, we emphasize the needto
   characterize correlations among integration processes,defect formation,
   and device performance-critical forguiding defect-minimizing process
   design. In addition,evaluating technologies against rigorous standards
   forlarge-scale uniformity (e.g., wafer-level deposition)
   andcost-effectiveness (e.g., compatibility with CMOS infras-tructure) is
   essential for transitioning 2-D transistors fromlaboratory prototypes to
   industrial manufacturing.},
