Pages = {4090-4095},
Month = {AUG},
Abstract = {Physical unclonable function (PUF) represents a widely utilized
   technology within the domain of hardware security. Memristor is a common
   source of entropy in PUFs due to its variable resistance during
   switching. However, in a memristor crossbar array, the issue of sneak
   path current necessitates the sacrifice of area to incorporate
   transistors as selectors. In light of the aforementioned considerations,
   this article proposes a new PUF scheme based on a Pt/TaOX/Al2O3/TiN
   self-rectifying memristor (SRM), which is capable of attaining a high
   memory density of 4 F-2/bit and exhibits a nonlinear I-V characteristic
   curve, thereby effectively mitigating the sneak path current issue.
   Based on the results of the electrical performance test and mechanism
   analysis, a simulation model of the SRM prepared was established, and a
   matching entropy source extraction PUF circuit was designed. The
   simulation results demonstrate that the PUF key exhibits 50.1\%
   uniqueness, 49.6\% uniformity, and 99\% reliability. Our work provides a
   promising avenue for the application of SRM in hardware security and
   effectively advances the development of PUF.},
