Volume = {22},
Number = {2},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {Thread-Level Speculation (TLS) utilizes speculative parallelization to
   accelerate hard-to-parallelize serial codes on multi-cores. As the
   heterogeneous multi-core architecture is becoming ubiquitous, it
   presents an opportunity for TLS to reorganize little cores for the
   acceleration of these serial codes instead of a big core with similar or
   more area and power. However, previous TLS designs significantly suffer
   from extended hardware overhead and costly speculative forwarding. We
   present LitTLS, a lightweight TLS design with versioning caches to
   eliminate significant extended hardware overhead by storing versions in
   caches without speculative write buffers and memory undo-logs.
   Additionally, LitTLS introduces the Speculative Address Table, a novel
   component to accelerate speculative forwarding with a central structure
   to trace memory dependencies. Evaluations on four little cores show that
   LitTLS achieves an average performance speedup of 2.87x compared to a
   little core, outperforming a big core by 94\% with similar area and less
   power. The extended area size is only 0.07 mm2, and the maximum increase
   in dynamic power consumption is limited to 0.3\%, compared to four
   little cores.},
