Abstract = {The interest in the preparation of nanoscale oxide semiconductors of the
   In-Ga-Zn-O system is due to the rapid development of printed electronics
   and solar cells. There are numerous methods for the synthesis of oxide
   nanomaterials (coprecipitation, sol-gel, self-combustion, etc),
   including a relatively novel reverse micelle synthesis. In the present
   study, In2O3, Ga2O3, ZnO oxides are prepared using self-combustion, and
   reverse micelle synthesis. The phase composition and morphology of the
   obtained oxides are described. The average size of their particles is
   determined from the XRD data, scanning electron microscopy, specific
   surface area, and dynamic light scattering of the suspensions of
   prepared powders. It is shown that the finest oxide powders are obtained
   by the reverse micelle synthesis.},
